.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 909 PM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026
(Tonight through Sunday)

While there were some light showers Friday, the lightning never
happened and that threat is now over. The 00Z sounding measured
1.32" of precipitable water. That`s well over the 90th percentile
(1.04"), and near the maximum recorded for this date (1.38").
Despite this near record moisture, dry air is moving in from the
northwest remarkably fast. The ECMWF ensemble mean predicts the PW
will drop from 1.38" Friday to around 0.5" Saturday. That means
the atmospheric moisture is decreasing from well over the 90th
percentile all the way down to the 10th percentile in only 12-24
hours.

As a result of the much drier air, the skies will be mostly clear
Saturday. The marine layer is trying to re-organize, but any
stratus will likely be patchy and evaporate in the mid to late morning.
The mid and high level clouds are already clearing and will not
return anytime soon. Despite the sunshine, temperatures will
remain several degrees cooler than normal this weekend.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 909 PM PDT Fri Aug 28 2026
(Sunday night through next Friday)

Upper level troughing will persist through the week, with a few
embedded shortwave features bringing some early rain to the
Pacific Northwest. In the Bay Area, rain is less likely, but the
cooling trend will continue through at least the middle of the
week. Max temperatures will be roughly 10-15 degrees below normal
from Monday through Wednesday. The 850 mb temperature is expected
to drop below the 25th percentile (16.1C) by Tuesday morning.

